    "case_id": "233",
    "case_summary": "A 10-day-old, 40-kg male Chianina calf was referred for evaluation of progressive dyspnea, lethargy, and recumbency. On presentation, temperature was 38.5 °C; heart rate, 170 beats/min with regular rhythm; respiratory rate, 60 breaths/min; jugular pulses were present and mucous membranes were cyanotic, especially after brief exertion. Cardiac auscultation revealed a grade IV/VI right apical systolic murmur and a more pronounced heartbeat on the right precordium. Lung sounds were harsh bronchovesicular. Electrocardiography showed sinus tachycardia at 170 beats/min. Transthoracic echocardiography demonstrated severe dilation of the right atrium and right ventricle, a markedly hypoplastic left ventricle with a severely reduced mitral valve orifice, a 16 mm interatrial defect in the upper septum, and a 6 mm muscular interventricular defect in the dorsal septum. An oblique parasternal long-axis view revealed two great vessels arising in parallel from the right ventricle, differentiated by coronary arteries from one vessel and by left and right pulmonary arteries from the other. Color-flow Doppler showed severe tricuspid regurgitation and bidirectional shunting at both septal defects. Agitated saline contrast injected via the left jugular vein produced bubbles in the left atrium and ventricle and exiting the right ventricle through both great vessels.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. double-outlet right ventricle\nB. Tetralogy of Fallot was considered because it involves aortic override and pulmonic stenosis, but was excluded as “in\nC. Complete transposition of the great vessels was considered among ventriculoarterial anomalies, but was distinguished by\nD. Single common arterial trunk was considered, but was excluded since “aorta and pulmonary artery were differentiated by\nE. Solitary arterial trunk (absent pulmonary artery) was considered, but was excluded as “pulmonary artery can be absent\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
